  "summary": "Troy, NY Mayor Carmella Mantello vetoed Local Law 3, a City Council measure that would have shortened Flock ALPR data retention from 30 days to 72 hours, required judicial authorization for data sharing, and limited camera searches to felony investigations. Mantello argued the law hampers police investigations and exceeds constitutional privacy protections, while Council President Sue Steele said the council plans to override the veto on October 22.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Local Law 3 reduced how long police can store license plate data from 30 days to 72 hours and restricts the sharing of the data without explicit judicial authorization.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "This law is biased in favor Of criminal defendants. It significantly undermines the ability of the Troy Police Department to investigate and solve serious violent crimes committed in the City.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    },
    {
      "quote": "What prevents misuse of Flock data is professional police management and standards, not amateurish regulations drafted by non-professionals and driven by feverish phantom fears.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "The City Council will override the veto on October 22.",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    }
  ],
